+# Attempt-based completion: worked measurement contract
+
+This is an illustrative M3 policy to adapt with the feature owner, not a universal analytics-tool default. Preserve Goal → Question → Metric → collection traceability. Reuse an existing reliable lifecycle source before adding events; a page view or displayed error is not evidence of a distinct attempt.
+
+## Unit, events and identity
+
+- Unit: one accepted core-action attempt, keyed by `(org_id, attempt_id)`. Define the exact accepted-start boundary for the feature. Generate the ID once there and retain it through technical retries; an explicit new attempt after a terminal outcome gets a new ID.
+- `feature_action_started` records that boundary. `feature_action_completed`, `feature_action_failed` and `feature_action_cancelled` record terminal success, failure and user cancellation. Choose one authoritative producer for lifecycle outcomes; do not add frontend copies of an existing server event.
+- Every lifecycle record has `event_id`, `org_id`, `attempt_id`, `event_at` (UTC occurrence time), `version` and the permitted segment fields. `user_id` is needed only for an explicitly user-based metric such as M1. Collect identifiers without copying action payloads or sensitive content.
+- Deduplicate identical re-deliveries by `event_id`. Conflicting payloads under the same ID are data-quality errors. Count one start and at most one outcome per attempt; a conflicting later terminal outcome is a contract violation, not another completion. Late delivery does not change the event's occurrence time.
+
+## Cohort and calculation
+
+Example policy: starts during `[2026-10-01T00:00:00Z, 2026-10-02T00:00:00Z)`; success must occur from the start through **24 hours** after that start, inclusive. Report at or after `2026-10-03T00:00:00Z`, with the ingestion watermark past the required horizon. Set the real period, horizon, timezone and lateness allowance for each feature.
+
+Denominator `N`: distinct eligible started attempts in that cohort. Numerator `S`: those same attempts with a valid success within their horizon. **M3 = S / N**. Count cancellations and failures in `N` and not `S`; report their counts separately. A started attempt with no terminal event by its mature deadline is a timeout/abandonment in this policy. A later completion remains outside this numerator. Do not remove cancellations or failures to improve the rate; an alternative policy requires a separate definition and comparison.
+
+State the eligibility/exclusion rules before collection (for example synthetic/test traffic) and apply them to numerator and denominator together. Starts exactly at the period end belong to the next cohort. Orphan outcomes, invalid timestamps, missing IDs, success before start, conflicting outcomes and missing coverage must be surfaced; do not silently manufacture starts or treat missing telemetry as failures.
+
+## Availability and validation
+
+- `N = 0` with verified coverage: value **null / no eligible attempts**, not 0%.
+- Required sources not instrumented, unavailable or unverified: value **null / not instrumented or unknown coverage**, not zero usage.
+- Cohort or watermark not mature: mark provisional; do not present an incomplete rate as a final result.
+- Contradictory identity/state records: flag data-quality failure before publishing a definitive rate.
+
+Test with synthetic starts, successes, failures, cancellation, timeout, duplicate delivery, late success, zero attempts, unavailable instrumentation and an immature cohort. Verify every Q has an M, every M has a collection/query contract, and every event has a purpose. Inspect exact query/tool units: a user-based funnel is not this attempt-based rate.
+
+Primary background (consulted 2026-10-15): [Basili, Caldiera and Rombach, GQM](https://www.cs.umd.edu/~basili/publications/technical/T89.pdf); [accessible author-paper copy](https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~sme/CSC444F/handouts/GQM-paper.pdf); [Amplitude's documented funnel units/windows](https://amplitude.com/docs/analytics/charts/funnel-analysis/funnel-analysis-how-amplitude-computes-conversions). The exact attempt policy above is a local worked example, not a rule attributed to those sources.
